---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { withBase } from "../lib/url";
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="CausewayAI brings 25 years of enterprise engineering experience to forward-deployed AI adoption — helping businesses operationalize AI without breaking how they operate."
  current="about"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">About CausewayAI</p>
      <h1>Named for the bridge, built on the work.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A causeway is a raised road built across water or unstable ground — a deliberate,
        engineered path where none existed before. That's what we build for enterprises trying
        to get from "we should use AI" to "AI is part of how we operate": a solid, engineered
        path, not a leap of faith.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <div>
          <h2>Why we exist</h2>
          <p>
            Most enterprises don't have an AI problem — they have an operating model problem.
            They can run a pilot. What's hard is turning that pilot into something IT will
            support, security will approve, compliance will sign off on, and the business can
            actually depend on. That gap is where CausewayAI works.
          </p>
          <p>
            We're not a strategy firm and we don't hand off a roadmap and leave. We embed
            engineers directly with your team, write code inside your systems, and bring
            hands-on discipline to how AI gets adopted — grounded in decades of experience
            in exactly the kind of regulated, high-stakes environments where "move fast and
            break things" was never an option.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2>What 25 years looks like</h2>
          <p>
            Our team's background spans Retail IT, Life Sciences, Food Services,
            Pharmaceuticals, Insurance, and Cybersecurity — industries where operational
            reliability, security, and regulatory scrutiny are constants, not edge cases.
          </p>
          <p>
            That experience shapes how we approach AI: we default to guardrails, auditability,
            and clear ownership, because we've seen what happens in enterprise environments when
            those things are missing. AI doesn't change that discipline — it raises the stakes
            on it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">How we work</p>
        <h2>Forward-deployed, not front-loaded.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>We embed, not advise from a distance</h3>
          <p>Our engineers work inside your tools, your codebase, and your review process — alongside your team, not around it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>We build for the audit, not just the demo</h3>
          <p>Every system we help build is designed with the assumption that someone in security or compliance will eventually ask hard questions about it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>We leave your team stronger</h3>
          <p>Knowledge transfer is part of the engagement, not an afterthought — the goal is a team that can own and extend what we built together.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cta-band">
        <h2>Want to know if we're the right fit?</h2>
        <p>The best way to find out is a conversation, not a pitch deck.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a href={withBase("/contact")} class="btn btn-accent">Get in touch</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
